We'll update this post as developments happen.
- The clock is now ticking after Donald Trump said yesterday that if Iran does not "fully open" the Strait of Hormuz within 48 hours, the US "will hit and obliterate their power plants";
- Iran today has responded with threats of its own, warning of four ways it will retaliate if the US leader follows through;
- One of Trump's top officials tried to defend the president and his rhetoric but said the US will do "whatever it takes" to achieve its war goals;
- Sir Keir Starmer is chairing an emergency COBRA meeting tomorrow on the war's cost-of-living impact, Sky News understands;
- Lebanon's president has claimed Israel is preparing for a "ground invasion" after it struck bridges in the country's south during its fight against Hezbollah;
- Meanwhile, various Tehran officials have insisted that the vital waterway remains open - except to vessels linked to its enemies;
- Israel has claimed Iran has long-range missiles that "can reach London, Paris or Berlin";
- It comes after more than 180 Israelis were injured in two strikes on Saturday evening, in the southern cities of Arad and Dimona;
- The attacks evaded Israel's air defences, raising serious questions as they hit near one of the country's main nuclear sites;
- The strikes were retaliation after Iran's own Natanz nuclear enrichment site was targeted;
- And finally, two Iranian ballistic missiles were fired at the Diego Garcia military base used by the UK and US on Friday night. But neither missile hit their intended target;
- This followed the UK saying it would allow US access to bases in the Middle East for "collective self-defence".
